      "title": "Occupation first",
      "narration": "Disability underwriting starts with the job, not the body. Insurers sort occupations into classes by the physical demands, the hazards and how easily a disabled worker could keep working. An attorney or accountant falls in the best class, with access to true own-occupation definitions, the longest benefit periods and the highest issue limits. Rosa, an electrician, sits in a middle class: her work is physical and hazardous, so her definition may be modified, her benefit period shorter and her premium higher. Some occupations, such as commercial fishing or stunt work, are declined entirely.",
